its ascent is a tough outing despite the distance - it lacks a formal path and is very rough going at times - usually with lots of livestock present. It can be very overgrown in places, and very boggy in others; the descent route is a little more straightforward  The neighbouring summit of Easter Cairn is the site of an old fort, and the views are extensive.', 56.1148, -3.57419),
('1f0456d6-f468-5212-a601-21e668c7f01e', 'Rubha nam Faing coastal walk, Portnahaven', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/islay-jura/rubha-na-faing.shtml', 6.5, 45, 2.0, 'Explore the coastline north of picturesque Portnahaven, with both pebble and sandy beaches, deep geos (inlets), and plenty of seals and birdlife along the way.', 55.6776, -6.5082),
('1f19b033-2e0b-5e79-85a9-502e95030226', 'Loch an Iasaich, Attadale', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/torridon/lochaniasaich.shtml', 11.5, 343, 4.0, 'This straightforward walk heads up an attractive strath before climbing a hydro road. It then follows old tracks through forestry, giving good views of the surrounding mountains. Hydro track construction has scarred the climb but Loch an Iasaich is a peaceful spot before the route descends through woodland and back along the valley with the opportunity to visit the impressive Attadale Gardens.', 57.3914, -5.45538),
('1fd65512-53df-5210-b0a6-d2e9e2469ed6', 'Ring of St Mary''s Loch', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/borders/st-marys-loch.shtml', 14.75, 172, 5.5, 'Set in a remote location half way along the Selkirk to Moffat road, St Mary''s Loch ranks amongst the most attractive lochs in the Borders. Recently opened paths enable walkers to make a complete circuit of the loch with only short sections on the road - a fine walk. The route as described here includes a couple of detours to visit an old kirkyard and a ruined tower house - these could be omitted if required to reduce the length of the walk by 2.5km or so.', 55.4719, -3.2072),
('1fe6b256-6183-553c-be09-92f9279cc666', 'Streap, near Glenfinnan', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/fortwilliam/streap.shtml', 18.5, 1197, 9.0, 'Streap is a magnificent mountain, just 5m below Munro height but ranking amongst the finest Corbetts. This route approachs it along a grand, undulating ridge with a dramatic final arête leading up to the summit. The route continues over the subsidiary peak of Streap Comhlaidh before a long, steep grassy descent. The route gives a more strenuous day than a glance at the map might suggest.', 56.8636, -5.3982),
('20b96adb-77f0-5bbf-a97a-1da18cef4dd5', 'Abernethy Glen and Castle Law, Abernethy', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/perthshire/abernethy-glen.shtml', 4.6, 250, 2.5, 'Climb the ancient round tower in the heart of the pretty village of Abernethy before heading off on this short circular route around the local glen. The walk includes the optional steep ascent of Castle Law - the site of an iron age fort and a superb viewpoint.', 56.332, -3.3125),
('20e32c93-a783-5012-85f5-b7c383e09d41', 'New Luce and the Caves of Kilhern', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/galloway/new-luce.shtml', 9.0, 105, 3.0, 'This circular walk explores a quiet and somewhat neglected part of Dumfries and Galloway. It combines a section of the Southern Upland Way over the moors with minor roads to make a circuit from the small village of New Luce.', 54.9423, -4.8508),
('20ed71a5-5268-5b4b-904a-176b23d9cac4', 'Tain links circuit', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/ullapool/tain-links.shtml', 3.5, 38, 1.0, 'This short and easy walk explores the environs of Tain, the oldest Royal Burgh in Scotland. It takes in a range of historic buildings as well as wandering down to the shore of the Dornoch Firth.', 57.8124, -4.0554),
('2121d88b-0e78-5376-8203-24abf8f703ee', 'Mull of Galloway', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/galloway/mull-of-galloway.shtml', 10.5, 185, 4.5, 'The southernmost tip of Scotland, the Mull of Galloway has a splendid lighthouse, bustling seabird colonies, dramatic cliff scenery and stunning views. This walk explores the coast on both sides of the peninsula. A shorter option is also given, returning along the road from West Tarbet which reduces the walk to 3.5km.', 54.6354, -4.86),
('217774ee-9f14-5851-9375-94101022023b', 'Bonnet Laird Walk: Carloway to Callanish', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/outer-hebrides/bonnet-laird.shtml', 14.5, 313, 5.0, 'This linear walk was created by the local estate, owned by a Community Trust (Urras Oighreachd Chàrlabhaigh). It crosses boggy moorland with some extensive views over the moors and coastline, as runs from Carloway to Callanish. Both ends are on a road with public transport. The Callanish stones are 1.3km from the end, whilst the Dun Carloway broch is 1.6km from the start (both along roads).', 58.2776, -6.7742),
('21d2d23f-fcc3-5143-bd84-cfbf07b99fb6', 'Cuan coast and moor circuit, Isle of Seil', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/argyll/cuan-seil.shtml', 6.0, 128, 2.5, 'This varied walk follows the coast and raised beach to Cuan where the ferry crosses from Seil to Luing. The route returns via Kilbrandon Church with its impressive stained glass, and a moorland path.', 56.2909, -5.6333),
('220fa459-6f6b-5d9f-b655-29d4dc28ed7d', 'Craignish Point, near Ardfern', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/argyll/craignish-point.shtml', 3.0, 87, 1.5, 'A short coastal walk to Craignish Point overlooking the Sound of Jura, Scarba and the Corryvreckan whirlpool. A good place for otter spotting or to watch the sunset.', 56.1429, -5.6031),
('220ffedf-8b65-56f0-974b-fe3dda3f25ff', 'Minn beach and Kettla Ness, West Burra', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/shetland/banna-min.shtml', 7.75, 145, 3.0, 'Minn Beach is one of Shetland''s most beautiful sweeps of sand, set along the west side of a stunning tombolo at Banna Min, leading out to Kettla Ness, the southernmost part of West Burra. The peninsula beyond the beach gives enjoyable walking above a wild coastline.', 60.0619, -1.3407),
('222ede64-a991-5fd3-bf01-fc408694d47d', 'The Knock of Crieff, Crieff', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/perthshire/knock.shtml', 6.1, 200, 2.0, 'The Knock is the wooded hill that stands guard over the town of Crieff. This circular route begins by climbing to the top for excellent and extensive views on the boundary of the Highlands and Lowlands; it is the just the first part of this very pleasant circular route.', 56.3821, -3.8415),
('22424eb1-9953-5207-b8dd-7545b39e4ff7', 'Inverawe and Loch Etive, near Taynuilt', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/argyll/inverawe.shtml', 3.5, 54, 1.5, 'This beautiful circuit from the Inverawe Country Park begins with a fine riverside stretch before venturing down to the beautiful shores of Loch Etive.', 56.4361, -5.2123),
('2252b409-70d6-5a94-91bf-a8360fb4ab86', 'Kintra and Braighchreaich', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/mull/kintra.shtml', 4.0, 25, 1.5, 'This short walk across croft land (best kept dog-free) leads from the pretty bay and cottages at Kintra to a couple of deserted villages dating back to the time of the Clearances.', 56.3439, -6.3483),
('2279738d-e0ee-56ff-be44-4d521e43af0d', 'North Kessock and Kilmuir circuit', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/lochness/north-kessock.shtml', 9.0, 242, 4.5, 'This scenic circuit is just over the Kessock bridge from the Highland capital, Inverness. It runs along an attractive shortline to reach the hidden hamlet of Kilmuir before returning along the flanks of Ord Hill.', 57.5021, -4.2463),
('22972b4b-6e79-5442-afb5-b1ff9191883a', 'Glasgow City Centre and the Green', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/glasgow/glasgow-green.shtml', 5.0, 19, 2.0, 'This city walk takes in a section of cyclepath alongside the Clyde and the parkland of Glasgow Green and the People''s Palace before returning via part of the city centre - including George Square.', 55.8606, -4.2587),
('2297d3bb-c0b6-5f8b-8181-c7e4342be75d', 'Benarty Hill from Vane Farm', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/perthshire/benarty-vane-farm.shtml', 7.1, 300, 3.0, 'This superb walk climbs up the steep slopes behind the RSPB''s Vane Farm visitor centre to reach the summit of Benarty Hill. There are truly stunning views over Loch Leven, its surrounding hills and islands. The return is varied by coming down over Vane Hill.', 56.176, -3.3556),
('22c8a8ee-0249-5177-bbb4-76849f7b9ddb', 'Plodda Falls, near Tomich', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/lochness/Ploddafalls.shtml', 2.5, 94, 1.0, 'Plodda Falls is the highest and most spectacular waterfall in the area, over forty metres high; the falls are a great finale to this pleasant walk through a magnificent forest of Douglas Fir.', 57.2726, -4.8548),
('22d53ca2-5c35-5de0-921a-a7e673407ebc', 'Ferry Wood and the Ord, Lairg', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/sutherland/lairg.shtml', 5.0, 85, 2.0, 'This route is a combination of two short waymarked walks from the Ferrycroft Visitor Centre, an exhibition giving the chance to find out about the natural history of the area. The walks visit the shores of a loch, a hill-top viewpoint and some archaeological remains.', 58.0218, -4.4066),
('238fcacb-613f-5dd6-ab3a-f98b18422cdb', 'Fintry Bay, Great Cumbrae', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/glasgow/fintry-bay-cumbrae.shtml', 7.5, 45, 2.5, 'This idyllic walk more than repays the minor effort involved. Climbing out of Millport, it soon reveals superb views across Bute and the Firth of Clyde to the rocky north Arran hills. The rougher section by the coastline can be avoided.', 55.7511, -4.9327),
('239725a1-8568-50ed-b3ab-cf51ef49014d', 'Lindores Loch circuit, Grange of Lindores', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/fife-stirling/lindores-loch.shtml', 6.0, 114, 2.5, 'A circuit around Lindores Loch, mainly through farmland, with good countryside views and a couple of short steep climbs. Some navigation is needed and care on the short section of main road.', 56.3364, -3.2027),
('23adebb4-a15b-56a8-baa2-ca7f5e15551a', 'A'' Chralaig and Mullach Fraoch-choire', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/kintail/Achralaig.shtml', 13.0, 1150, 8.0, 'A Chralaig is the highest of the mountains bordering the main road to Skye. The ascent is a steep grind but the ridge walk onto Mullach Fraoch-choire is as fine as any in Kintail.', 57.1596, -5.1647),
('23cb7871-18fe-5315-b7d2-25d6311112f1', 'Freswick to Dunscansby Head', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/sutherland/freswick.shtml', 8.25, 120, 2.5, 'A wild section of coastline with dramatic geos and cliffs, finishing at the amazing stacks and lighthouse of Dunscansby Head. This is a linear route described as a one-way walk; there is no parking at the start, but if you are not being dropped off then there is a regular bus from John o''Groats taking 10 minutes to reach the Skirza road end at Freswick; you can then return to John o''Groats by following the coast from Dunscansby Head, adding an extra 3km.', 58.594, -3.0864),
('24094f1e-47ee-5fcf-87ea-2e28b58f595c', 'Beinn Resipol from Ariundle', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/fortwilliam/beinn-resipol-ariundle.shtml', 15.25, 828, 7.0, 'A tougher alternative to the route from Resipole campsite, this tackles the Corbett from Ariundle, climbing to the east ridge over very rough and boggy ground but with excellent views on both the ascent and descent. Beinn Resipol itself is one of the great viewpoints of Scotland.', 56.7203, -5.5587),
('241a2056-6d03-5a50-af05-f03d4423edaa', 'Faraclett Head, Rousay', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/orkney/faraclett-head.shtml', 3.5, 87, 1.5, 'A coastal circular above cliffs to the highest point on Faraclett Head, a good place to spot terns and skuas.', 59.1737, -2.9714),
('242a2ed3-ee61-57cd-ba80-a10e8bb97049', 'Tobar na Slainte, Shandwick', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/ullapool/tobar-na-slainte.shtml', 3.0, 70, 1.0, 'This short coastal excursion visits an old healing well, passing excellent beaches along the way.', 57.751, -3.9181),
('2445804b-65c5-5723-80bd-faa280f24e47', 'Hirsel Country Park, Coldstream', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/borders/the-hirsel.shtml', 8.5, 65, 2.5, 'Explore Dunglass Wood on the Hirsel estate on the edge of Coldstream before passing the Hirsel itself - a magnificent country house. You may see the estate''s herd of Douglas pedigree Highland Cattle. There is a visitor centre and cafe just off route and toilets at the start.', 55.6505, -2.25534),
('2452897b-0040-5db0-8897-fb6422ba49f8', 'Cuairt Cromor, South Lochs', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/outer-hebrides/cuairt-cromor.shtml', 3.75, 131, 1.5, 'This short but challenging short circular walk from Cromor packs plenty of interest and has been waymarked by wooden posts. Note that near the end of the route it is necessary to wade the outflow of the loch - this is roughly knee deep and is over slippery rocks and broken mussel shells, so needs to be done still wearing footwear - even then it requires great care. A route with such an obstacle would not normally be featured on Walkhighlands but due to the waymarking and local promotion we feel it is better to provide a description so walkers know what to expect.', 58.1037, -6.4153),
('245e3c35-8aaf-59bf-b16f-e9e925fffbec', 'Ben Tirran by Loch Brandy and Wharral, Glen Clova', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/angus/ben-tirran.shtml', 14.5, 752, 6.0, 'Ben Tirran has a flat and rather featureless summit but is redeemed by its fine corries above Glen Clova. This walk visits both to make a circular route up Clova''s only Corbett summit.', 56.8442, -3.1054),
('24613494-1634-5e1c-9636-3570917024ce', 'Five Farms and Troup Head from Crovie', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/aberdeenshire/crovie-five-farms.shtml', 9.5, 285, 3.5, 'This superb circular walk climbs high above picturesque Crovie to explore the dramatic coastline of Troup Head and its spectacular bird colonies. This is described from the car park above Crovie but it can easily be started from Gardenstown by following the impressive coastal path from the far east end of Gardenstown.', 57.6764, -2.3248),
('2461927d-9ad6-5c76-bd3e-3d05f699ed80', 'River Ness and Caledonian Canal circuit, Inverness', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/lochness/Riverness.shtml', 11.5, 57, 4.5, 'A walk around Inverness on the banks of the River Ness, the Caledonian Canal and the coast. It makes an interesting walk whilst staying in the city, or as an easier option on a rainy day.', 57.4773, -4.227),
('2463ebab-6dac-5782-9296-64f09b4194ed', 'North Third Reservoir, near Stirling', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/fife-stirling/north-third.shtml', 4.75, 98, 2.0, 'This excellent moderate walk encircles the attractive North Third reservoir. The route climbs up to the top of the craggy escarpment of Lewis Hill with superb views before descending to cross the dam and continue around the shore.', 56.0686, -3.999),
('249d149d-8e8d-5e1b-88f8-bda8100ec23b', 'Oronsay island, Ullinish', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/skye/oronsay.shtml', 5.0, 155, 2.5, 'A visit to the tidal island is sheer delight. Stunning coastal scenery on the island - and its superb views - mean that this must rank as one of the best shorter walks on Skye. Be sure to check the tide times though or you could become stranded!', 57.3482, -6.4527),
('24a30671-e51d-57e2-8fb2-e9b3042666cb', 'Mountain lookout trail, Crianlarich', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/lochlomond/crianlarich.shtml', 2.0, 34, 0.75, 'This short walk from Crianlarich is ideal as an early evening leg stretcher. It heads up through the native community woodlands with to a viewpoint overlooking the surrounding mountains. At the end of the loop there is a chance to visit the banks of the River Fillan.', 56.3925, -4.6162),
('24d73dbd-1302-5cd5-be38-17ea6a4d7511', 'Tràigh Chornaig - The Green', 'https://www.walkhighlands.co.uk/islands/traigh-chornaig.shtml', 3.35, 10, 1.0, 'This short walk heads from Loch Bhasapoll - often busy with people enjoying watersports - out to the beautiful and secluded beach of Tràigh Chornaig - also known as the Green - on the northern coast of Tiree.', 56.5212, -6.9213)
